# PowerShell 5.1 reads .ps1 as ANSI when the file has no BOM. Keeping the
# BOM intact also keeps non-ASCII safe if any is ever added. So scripts are
# binary as far as git is concerned: no re-encoding, no eol normalisation.
*.ps1  -text
*.psd1 -text

# Shell scripts are LF ALWAYS, checkout included - the opposite of the .ps1
# rule above. A CRLF checkout on Windows is not a cosmetic difference there:
# the \r becomes part of every command and string, and the script dies on the
# first line with an unreadable error naming a character you cannot see.
*.sh   text eol=lf

# Docs and agent definitions are plain text, LF in the repository.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf
VERSION text eol=lf

# GitHub: do not count documentation as code in language stats
*.md linguist-documentation